{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 29,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.066,
            0.374,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.121,
            0.885,
            0.164
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Subtraction of fractions with the same fractional unit or denominator"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.178,
            0.845,
            0.201
          ],
          "content": "Brahmagupta’s method also applies when subtracting fractions!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.201,
            0.884,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "Let us start with the problem of subtracting $\\frac { 4 } { 7 }$ from ${ \\frac { 6 } { 7 } } ,$ i.e., what is $\\frac { 6 } { 7 } - \\frac { 4 } { 7 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.274,
            0.885,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": "To solve this problem, we can again use the rectangular strips. In both fractions, the fractional unit is the same, i.e., ${ \\frac { 1 } { 7 } } .$ . Let us first represent the bigger fraction using a rectangular strip model as shown:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.353,
            0.365,
            0.698,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_29_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.476,
            0.884,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "Each shaded part represents ${ \\frac { 1 } { 7 } } .$ Now, we need to subtract ${ \\frac { 4 } { 7 } } .$ To do this let us remove 4 of the shaded parts:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.541,
            0.932,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_29_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.814,
            0.672,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "So, we are left with 2 shaded parts, i.e., $\\frac { 6 } { 7 } - \\frac { 4 } { 7 } = \\frac { 2 } { 7 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.853,
            0.717,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": "Try doing this same exercise using the number line."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.941,
            0.117,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
